| # | Item | Notes | Builder | State |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A** | **Bugs from the video** (`fix/ide-bugs`) | | | |
| U1 | Answers appear without a key press | **Confirmed bug.** `pump()` runs after drawing (`mpp-ide/src/app.rs:594-602`). When a result arrives in `worker.poll()` (`doc.rs:391-397`) and nothing else is pending, `pump` returns `None` and **no repaint is requested**, so the answer only shows on the next input. Fix: request a repaint whenever `poll()` delivers a result, and while a job is running, schedule `request_repaint_after` at the debounce. Test: type `$$ 1+2 = ? $$`, then stop sending input, run frames only; the answer must appear. Tests use the real loop with `pump`, not `settle()` (`doc.rs:571`). | Sonnet | done (988e77c). Not reproducible as described: one more frame always followed a result. Hardened anyway (`pump` returns zero wait when anything arrives) with a real-event-loop test, `tests/replay.rs`. Ben's lines never answered because they weren't maths at all (no `$$`): that is U12/U16. |
| U2 | The caret never moves by itself | The caret is a byte offset mapped again to a `CaretStop` after every rebuild (`page/mod.rs:305-333`). On an empty line there are only "gap stops" at x=0 in the middle of the gap between blocks (`page/build.rs:78-88, 127-139`). When the answer arrives, blocks grow notes underneath (`build.rs:1497-1543`, `view.rs:947-957`) and the stop moves. Fix: (a) an empty or new line gets a real line of its own with a real caret stop, at the line's own height; (b) the caret's stop is chosen by its offset **and** a sticky side (before/after) so a rebuild can't pick the next block's stop; (c) space for answers, notes and hints is reserved (see U16) so content below doesn't jump; (d) keep the viewport pinned to the caret's line across rebuilds. Test: replay the video's keystrokes (U27 script) and assert the caret's on-screen line after every frame equals the line of the last typed character. | Opus | done (lane A2: 988e77c, b5996dd, 7a6742f). Caret keeps its side across rebuilds; its line is pinned on screen. Reserved answer space left for U16. |
| U3 | Enter means a new line, and it stays one | Today one `\n` inside a paragraph is a soft break: the paragraph runs on to the next blank line (`mpp-syntax/src/document/block.rs:827-833`), so a line typed after one Enter joins the line above. Fix in the editor, not the grammar: **Enter ends the block** (the editor writes the blank line into the file), **Shift+Enter** is a soft line break inside a paragraph. What the user sees after Enter must match what the file means. Inside display maths, Enter starts a new maths line (an aligned row). Backspace at the start of a block joins it to the previous one in one step. Enter is undone in one step. | Sonnet | done (lane A3, bba715a on `wip/a3-input`) |
| U4 | Side column: one clear set of buttons | **Confirmed bug:** clicking the active icon sets `ws.side_visible=false` (`workspace/ui.rs:148-151`) but `tiles_ui` copies `side_visible` back from the tree later in the same frame (`layout/behavior.rs:913-923`), so the click does nothing. Also: the active highlight follows `ws.side_view`, which goes stale after closing a side tab (`behavior.rs:854-867`); `side_group` picks the parent of the first side pane found (`layout/tiles.rs:180-211`), so if a pane was dragged elsewhere Ctrl+B hides the wrong group; `tiles_ui` takes the tree out (`behavior.rs:776`), and anything calling `with_tree`/`ensure_tree` during `tree.ui` rebuilds it from disk and is then overwritten (`:363-389`, `:836`). Fix: make the activity bar the **only** way to switch side panels, VS Code style (click an icon = show that panel; click the active icon = hide the side column). Remove the panel tab strip, its × buttons and the ▸ overflow arrow from the side group (`behavior.rs:187` closable; egui_tiles tab-bar scroll arrow). Keep one source of truth for "which panel, visible or not" and set the tree from it. Tooltips on each icon. Tests click every icon in every order, plus Ctrl+B, plus dragging a pane out, and assert what's on screen. | Opus | done (lane A1, d0703ee + f9f55d9) |
| U4b | Files and attributes in separate side views | Ben, 2026-11-21: "I want to see different attributes on one window, and actual files on another. Maybe look for a filesystem view similar to vscode, with the other tiles added as other options." Today the Explorer panel mixes Open files, Tutorial and Outline in one list, which felt like too much. **Explorer icon = files only**, VS Code style: a folder tree with expand/collapse, file icons, the current file highlighted, single click opens, New file / New folder in the panel header, right-click rename / delete (confirmed) / reveal in Windows Explorer; an optional collapsible "Open editors" section at the top. **Every other kind of information gets its own icon and panel:** Search, Outline (headings and blocks), Definitions (names defined in the file with their current values, plus imports), Problems, Tutorial. All still draggable into the main area as tiles. Tests: Explorer shows only files and folders; each icon shows its own panel. | Opus | done (lane A1, f9f55d9 on `wip/a1-workspace`). Problems and Tutorial got their own icons too; section label kept as "OPEN FILES". |
| U5 | Start-up, Open and tabs respond straight away | In the video, nothing redrew for 8 s of clicks and Open recent until another click. Same root as U1: make sure every state change (file opened, panel switched, menu closed) requests a repaint. New files are named **Untitled-1, Untitled-2…** (never three tabs called "Untitled"). Clicking a file in Open files focuses that tab and puts the caret in it. Opening a file puts the caret at the end of its first line, so typing works at once. | Sonnet | done (lane A1, 7af3104) |
| U6 | Numbers never become lists | Typing `1.` turned the line into a numbered list and dropped the caret below it (99–113 s). Fix: `1. ` becomes a list item **only** when typed at the start of a prose line **and followed by a space**, and the first Ctrl+Z turns it back into `1. ` text. `1.3`, `1.`, `2.5x` and anything on a maths line are never lists. Same for `- ` and `* ` bullets (never on a maths line, where `-` and `*` are operators). | Sonnet | done (lane A3, bba715a). **Needs Ben:** grammar change, a lone `1.` is no longer a list (`mpp-syntax/src/document/list.rs`). |
| U7 | Headings work the way people type them | `heading_level` needs a space after the `#`s (`block.rs:453-464`, `docs/grammar.mpp:15`). Fix in the editor: typing `#` at the start of a line and then a letter inserts the space for you and the line becomes a heading straight away, with a one-time hint ("Heading 1 — Ctrl+Z for a plain #"). The grammar stays strict; the editor writes `# This…`. Also add Ctrl+1/2/3 = heading level, Ctrl+0 = body text. | Haiku | done (lane A3, bba715a). **Needs Ben:** Ctrl+0 is now body text, so zoom reset moved to Ctrl+Alt+0; hint shows once per open file. |
| U8 | A calm status bar | "Working…" flickers on nearly every key (`workspace/ui.rs:540-542`, `evaluating()` at `doc.rs:566`, 100 ms debounce at `layout/mod.rs:24`). Fix: show "Working…" only after a run has taken **more than 1 s**; otherwise leave the bar as it is. Count definitions from the evaluator's results (it said "0 definitions" after two). The problems count doesn't flip back and forth between keys: it changes only when a run finishes. | Haiku | done (lane A1, 5cbe75b). Could not reproduce "0 definitions" in a test; the count now comes from the evaluator definition lines. |
| U9 | Arrow keys go where you expect | Left from the start of a line went to an invisible gap between blocks. Fix: no caret stops in the gaps between blocks. Left at the start of a line → end of the previous line; Right at the end → start of the next; Up/Down keep the caret's x position (a "goal column") across lines of different heights; Home/End go to the line's ends; Ctrl+Home/End go to the document's ends. Inside maths the arrows walk the structure (U19). | Sonnet | done (lane A2, d2e4189) |
| U10 | Never "locked in" | When focused, the editor captures Tab and the arrow keys (`view.rs:231-243`) and grabs focus whenever nothing has it (`view.rs:224-226`). Fix: arrows at the edge of the document, or Esc when nothing is open, release normally; Tab is captured **only** while a template has empty boxes; clicking anywhere in the side column or menus takes focus there; the editor doesn't steal focus back. Esc always does exactly one thing, in this order: close a popup → leave the box → leave maths → nothing. | Sonnet | done (lane A2, 50adb79) |
| **B** | **Maths vs prose: the pivot** (`feat/maths-lines`) | | | |
| U11 | Every line is clearly Maths or Prose | **The pivot Ben asked for.** The page is built from blocks that are visibly one of two kinds. **Maths blocks (Ben, 2026-11-21: "make it clear what is a math block"):** each one sits in its **own outlined box** (thin border, rounded corners, light tint inside) so you can see exactly where it starts and ends, even with nothing typed in it yet. A run of maths lines shares one box. A small **∑ marker in the left margin**, maths font, and a coloured caret while you're in it. **Prose lines:** plain page, no marker, a normal black caret. Inline maths inside a sentence (`$…$`) gets the same tint as a rounded chip. Today there is no tint and no different caret (`paint_page`, `view.rs:839-980`; `in_math` from `page/mod.rs:290`, `build.rs:2324`). Clicking the margin marker (or Ctrl+M) flips a line between Maths and Prose; undo flips it back. In the file, maths lines are saved as `$$ … $$` blocks, so the format is unchanged. Tint, marker and caret colours come from the theme and pass WCAG in light and dark (rule 8). | Opus | done (lane B1, daca928; PR #15) |
| U12 | Starting maths is obvious and automatic | Today a line is maths only if it is wrapped in `$`/`$$` (`document/mod.rs:5`, `block.rs:413`), which nobody discovers. New: **(a) explicit ways in**, all equal: type `$` (LaTeX users), `=` or `:=` at the start of an empty line, **Alt+=** (Word users), **Ctrl+M**, or Insert → Maths line. **(b) Auto-detect when a line is finished** (Enter or the caret leaves it): a line becomes maths if it has `:=`, `≡`, or ends with `=` / `= ?`, or it is mostly symbols, digits and operators with at most one word and that word is a known function or a defined name (`x := 5`, `E = 2x + 5`, `1.32 + 5 =`, `sin(x)^2`). Lines with two or more ordinary English words stay prose. When auto-detect fires, the line slides into its maths band and a one-time hint says "Turned into maths — Ctrl+Z to keep as text". The first Ctrl+Z undoes only the detection. **(c) While typing the first line in a new empty file**, a faint hint on the empty line: "Type text, or start with `=` or `$` for maths". Typora's rule keeps money as prose: a dollar sign straight before a digit, or a dollar sign followed by a space, never starts maths (so prices like five dollars stay text). Add this to the parser too, with a test. | Opus | done (lane B1, 639d4ba; PR #15). **Needs Ben:** softened money rule; `$x$ is…` at a line start becomes a maths line. |
| U13 | Mode and position in the status bar | The status bar shows **Prose** or **Maths** next to the caret position, and inside maths the path to the caret: "Maths › fraction › denominator", "Maths › integral › upper bound". Clicking "Prose/Maths" flips the line (same as U11). | Haiku | done (lane B1, cae2e74; PR #15) |
| U14 | Box around what you're typing in | Inside maths, draw a thin outline around the innermost structure holding the caret (fraction part, root, exponent, bracket, integral bound) and a fainter one for its parent, in a colour that isn't the selection colour (rule 2). Empty boxes show a faint placeholder ("numerator", "exponent"). | Sonnet | done (lane B1, fe287dc; PR #15) |
| U15 | Maths looks like maths while you type | Single-letter variables italic; functions (`sin`, `log`, `max`), units, numbers and multi-letter names upright; spacing applied **as you type**, not on a later redraw (`+5` stayed tight until 70.6 s): thick space around `= < ≤ :=`, medium around binary `+ −`, none after a unary minus or between a function name and its bracket. Scripts 0.71× per level, min ~9 px. Maths lines get ~1.5 line height so fractions and scripts don't change line spacing as you type. Multi-row maths aligns on `=`. | Sonnet | done (lane B1, 159b344; PR #15) |
| U16 | Answers show up by themselves, in a fixed place | Today auto-answers exist only in `$$` display blocks and only after a repaint (U1); inline maths is never auto-evaluated (`mpp-eval/tests/auto_eval.rs:172`); `:=` is excluded (`mpp-eval/src/block/auto.rs:40-75`). New: **(a) a maths line that ends with `=` evaluates**, with no `?` needed (`1.321321312 + 5 =` → 6.321321312); `= ?`, `≈ ?` and `= ?!` keep their meaning. **(b) A definition shows its value** in grey after it when the value is a number (`E := 2x + 5` → `= 15`), never for plain number definitions (`x := 5`). **(c) Answers go in a reserved slot** at the right end of the line (or below for long ones). The slot is reserved as soon as the line ends in `=`, so the answer arriving moves nothing (rule 5). **(d) Stale answers stay, dimmed**, while a new one runs; errors appear in the same slot in red with a ⚠ and a one-line message, with the full message on hover. **(e) Answers are clearly not typed text:** their own colour, preceded by `=`, and the caret can't enter them. Click an answer to copy it; Ctrl+click inserts it at the caret. Inline `$…$` maths ending in `=` also gets an answer chip. | Opus | done (lane B2, e60ac6a; PR #15) |
| U17 | Definitions the way both groups write them | `:=` is always a definition (mathematicians). **`x = 5` on a maths line, where the left side is a single name that isn't defined yet, is also a definition** (coders, calculators), shown with the same subtle "defined" mark so the user can see it was read that way; `x = 5` when `x` is already defined is a check (✓ or ✗ in the answer slot). `==` is accepted as a check too. A hover on any name shows its current value and where it was defined; Ctrl+click jumps there. The status bar count ("2 definitions") is correct (U8). | Opus | done (lane B2, 6f5ef4a; PR #15). **Needs Ben:** when `x = 5` defines (see PR #15). |
| **C** | **Typing for mathematicians and coders** (`feat/two-inputs`) | | | |
